winewayland.drv: present a popup larger than the screen where the app put it, unstretched (Animate first launch)

Animate 2024's first launch showed a black window covering the screen; Escape was the only way out.
The window is Animate's first-launch overlay with the "New to Animate?" card: an owned WS_POPUP that
Animate sizes to its main window's area times its UI scale (6714x3557 px at 175% on a 3840x2160
screen; every input to that arithmetic matches what Windows returns). Two things in the Wayland
driver hid it:

1. Popups are xdg_popups whose positioner asks the compositor to SLIDE them on-screen. A popup
   larger than every output cannot fit, so the slide only moved it: KWin slid it up and left until
   its bottom-right corner met the screen's, showing empty backdrop while the card sat off-screen.
   Windows leaves a window where the app put it and clips it at the screen edge. The positioner now
   slides only along an axis where the popup fits on an output; menus and dropdowns still slide.

2. win32u clips a window surface to the desktop, so such a window's buffer is smaller than the
   window. The viewport source is clamped to the buffer but the destination was the full window, so
   the compositor stretched the buffer (1.17x by 1.74x here): the card was drawn down and to the
   right of where Animate hit-tests it, and its buttons did not respond. A buffer smaller than its
   window is now presented 1:1, both where the buffer is attached and where the surface is
   reconfigured. Windows that extend past the left or top of the desktop are not handled here.

Harness: preserved-fixes/harnesses/popup-oversize.c + popup-oversize-check.py. On 11.10-108 `big`
FAILS (both squares off-screen) and `edge` passes; patched, both pass (square aspect 1.01, spacing
4.04/2.04, want 1/4/2). Animate first launch: the card is centered and its buttons respond
(operator). Regression pass in the clean room (operator): Premiere 2025 dropdowns, context menu,
Export dialog, Captioneer panel; Lightroom Classic Import dialog, dropdowns, filmstrip menu;
Audition menus and New Audio File; InDesign menus and New Document.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
@@ -429,6 +429,10 @@
     BOOL resizing;
     struct wayland_window_config window;
     int content_width, content_height;
+    /* NEUTRON: size of the shm buffer last attached, in window px (0 before the first attach).
+     * win32u clips a window surface to the desktop, so a window larger than the desktop has a
+     * buffer SMALLER than its window; see wayland_surface_viewport_destination(). */
+    int buffer_width, buffer_height;
     /* NEUTRON: has this surface EVER held content? content_width is zeroed by
      * wayland_surface_clear_role() on every hide, so it cannot distinguish "was drawn, hidden,
      * re-shown" from "brand new, never painted". This one is never cleared. */
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -715,6 +715,22 @@
     ERR("Failed to assign subsurface role to wayland surface\n");
 }
 
+/* The largest logical width and height of any known output (0 when none is known yet). */
+static void wayland_outputs_max_logical_size(int *width, int *height)
+{
+    struct wayland_output *output;
+
+    *width = *height = 0;
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&process_wayland.output_mutex);
+    wl_list_for_each(output, &process_wayland.output_list, link)
+    {
+        if (!output->current.current_mode) continue;
+        *width = max(*width, output->current.logical_w);
+        *height = max(*height, output->current.logical_h);
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&process_wayland.output_mutex);
+}
+
 /* Build an xdg_positioner that places the popup at (config->x, config->y)
  * relative to the owner window's top-left, in surface-local coords. */
 static struct xdg_positioner *popup_create_positioner(struct wayland_surface *owner,
@@ -755,10 +771,30 @@
     xdg_positioner_set_size(xdg_positioner, width, height);
     /* Let the compositor slide the popup to keep it on-screen. Slide-only keeps
      * the size constant so it stays consistent with our fixed menu buffer and
-     * window geometry (flip/resize would change size/orientation). */
-    xdg_positioner_set_constraint_adjustment(xdg_positioner,
-        XDG_POSITIONER_CONSTRAINT_ADJUSTMENT_SLIDE_X |
-        XDG_POSITIONER_CONSTRAINT_ADJUSTMENT_SLIDE_Y);
+     * window geometry (flip/resize would change size/orientation).
+     *
+     * NEUTRON: but only along an axis where the popup fits on an output. A popup larger than every
+     * output cannot be brought on-screen by sliding; the slide only changes which part of it is
+     * visible, while Windows leaves a window exactly where the app put it and clips it at the
+     * screen edge. Animate's first-launch overlay (owned by the main window, sized to its area
+     * times the UI scale: 6714x3557 at 175%) was slid up and left until its bottom-right corner
+     * met the screen's, so only empty backdrop showed and the "New to Animate?" card sat
+     * off-screen. Menus and dropdowns, which fit, still slide. */
+    {
+        uint32_t adjust = 0;
+        int out_width, out_height;
+
+        wayland_outputs_max_logical_size(&out_width, &out_height);
+        if (!out_width || width <= out_width) adjust |= XDG_POSITIONER_CONSTRAINT_ADJUSTMENT_SLIDE_X;
+        if (!out_height || height <= out_height) adjust |= XDG_POSITIONER_CONSTRAINT_ADJUSTMENT_SLIDE_Y;
+        if (adjust != (XDG_POSITIONER_CONSTRAINT_ADJUSTMENT_SLIDE_X |
+                       XDG_POSITIONER_CONSTRAINT_ADJUSTMENT_SLIDE_Y))
+            TRACE("popup %dx%d is larger than every output (%dx%d); slide %s%s only\n",
+                  width, height, out_width, out_height,
+                  (adjust & XDG_POSITIONER_CONSTRAINT_ADJUSTMENT_SLIDE_X) ? "x" : "",
+                  (adjust & XDG_POSITIONER_CONSTRAINT_ADJUSTMENT_SLIDE_Y) ? "y" : "");
+        xdg_positioner_set_constraint_adjustment(xdg_positioner, adjust);
+    }
 
     return xdg_positioner;
 }
@@ -943,6 +979,8 @@
 
     surface->content_width = 0;
     surface->content_height = 0;
+    surface->buffer_width = 0;
+    surface->buffer_height = 0;
 
     wl_display_flush(process_wayland.wl_display);
 }
@@ -957,6 +995,8 @@
  */
 static BOOL wayland_surface_get_frame_spill_rect(struct wayland_surface *surface,
                                                  RECT *rect);
+static BOOL wayland_surface_viewport_destination(struct wayland_surface *surface,
+                                                 int *width, int *height);
 
 void wayland_surface_attach_shm(struct wayland_surface *surface,
                                 struct wayland_shm_buffer *shm_buffer,
@@ -1039,6 +1079,16 @@
                                wl_fixed_from_int(win_height));
     }
 
+    surface->buffer_width = shm_buffer->width;
+    surface->buffer_height = shm_buffer->height;
+    if (!wayland_surface_get_frame_spill_rect(surface, &spill))
+    {
+        int dest_width, dest_height;
+
+        if (wayland_surface_viewport_destination(surface, &dest_width, &dest_height))
+            wp_viewport_set_destination(surface->wp_viewport, dest_width, dest_height);
+    }
+
     surface->content_width = win_width;
     surface->content_height = win_height;
     surface->ever_had_content = TRUE;
@@ -1488,6 +1538,39 @@
 }
 
 /**********************************************************************
+ *          wayland_surface_viewport_destination
+ *
+ * NEUTRON: the viewport destination for a window whose buffer is smaller than the window.
+ *
+ * win32u clips a window surface to the desktop, so a window that extends past it gets a buffer
+ * covering only the part inside it (from the window's top-left). The viewport source is clamped to
+ * that buffer, but the destination was the FULL window size, so the compositor stretched the
+ * buffer over the whole window: Animate's first-launch overlay (6714x3557 px, buffer 5760x2048)
+ * was magnified 1.17x by 1.74x, its card drawn down and to the right of where Animate hit-tests
+ * it, so the buttons did not respond. Present the buffer 1:1 instead, as Windows shows such a
+ * window clipped at the screen edge. Returns FALSE when the buffer covers the whole window (the
+ * normal case: the destination stays the window size).
+ *
+ * Assumes the buffer starts at the window's top-left, which holds for a window that extends past
+ * the right or bottom of the desktop; one that starts left of or above it is not handled here.
+ */
+static BOOL wayland_surface_viewport_destination(struct wayland_surface *surface,
+                                                 int *width, int *height)
+{
+    int win_width = surface->window.rect.right - surface->window.rect.left;
+    int win_height = surface->window.rect.bottom - surface->window.rect.top;
+
+    if (!surface->buffer_width || !surface->buffer_height) return FALSE;
+    if (surface->buffer_width >= win_width && surface->buffer_height >= win_height) return FALSE;
+
+    wayland_surface_coords_from_window(surface, min(win_width, surface->buffer_width),
+                                       min(win_height, surface->buffer_height), width, height);
+    *width = max(1, *width);
+    *height = max(1, *height);
+    return TRUE;
+}
+
+/**********************************************************************
  *          wayland_surface_reconfigure_size
  *
  * Sets the surface size with viewporter
@@ -1826,6 +1909,16 @@
         break;
     }
 
+    if (!content)
+    {
+        int dest_width, dest_height;
+
+        if (wayland_surface_viewport_destination(surface, &dest_width, &dest_height))
+        {
+            width = dest_width;
+            height = dest_height;
+        }
+    }
     wayland_surface_reconfigure_size(surface, width, height);
 
     return TRUE;
